### 3. Fresh-tree reproduction at both revisions (measured)

`reference/*/gen` is gitignored; the tests read the on-disk trees live. To make
"passing at HEAD" mean "passing on fresh output", I regenerated the trees
myself instead of trusting the on-disk state:

- **Pre-fix worktree** (`git worktree add` at `ec4c5c2d`, clean checkout,
  `.haxelib` dev-markers replicated): regenerated ts, rust, kotlin, swift, dart
  (`haxe examples/<t>.hxml`, all exit 0; 39–70 s each) and ran the scoped
  five files: **exit 1, 88 pass / 6 fail / 670 expect** (`prefix-five-files.exit/.log`).
  The six failures, by name, are exactly the six §4 reds:
  - `compiler boundary ... RustExpr.hx carries no banned identifier`
  - `loop structure > reference/ts/gen carries no functional iteration...`
  - `loop structure > reference/rust/gen carries no functional iteration...`
  - `arithmetic helpers generated tree > Rust inlines arithmetic helpers...`
  - `sorted dataClass key generated trees > pins resident comparators`
  - `sorted key domains generated tree > Rust generated tree widens an int capacity bound...`
- **Post-fix (HEAD `4c292c64`)**: same five trees regenerated (all exit 0;
  `regen-*.log`, `regen-summary.txt`), scoped run exit 0 (94/0/705, twice).
- **Tree currency**: each regenerated tree is byte-identical to the on-disk
  tree the scoped tests read (`diff -rq` empty; `diff-<t>-regen.txt`).
- **In-flight patch isolation**: during my session another seat applied an
  uncommitted `SwiftExpr.hx` patch (mtime 13:56:55 local, mid-run). It is
  compiled only under the swift hxml, so ts/rust/kotlin/dart regenerations
  reflect the committed compiler state. For swift, a **pure-HEAD worktree**
  regeneration (committed state only) is byte-identical to both the
  shared-worktree regeneration and the on-disk tree
  (`regen-swift-pure-head.log`, `diff-swift-purehead-vs-*.txt`) — i.e. the
  scoped pass holds against committed-compiler output.
- **Consistency check**: pre-fix vs post-fix rust-f32 trees differ in exactly
  the nine files the `50a95377` emitter changes touch
  (`diff-rustf32-prefix-vs-purehead.txt`); on-disk rust-f32 matches the
  pure-HEAD regeneration byte-for-byte (`diff-rustf32-snapshot-vs-purehead.txt`).

This independently reproduces the seat's upgrade claim ("all six reproduced on
fresh output at `ec4c5c2d`"; its logs `dc-warn/out/baseline-red-fix/evidence/`
show the same 88/6/670 and 94/0/705).

